{
  "0": "Across all four datasets, replace each supervised GRU-state readout with 0.8*h_t + 0.2*m_t, where m_t is the normalized exponentially weighted mean of real-item states through t, with a four-interaction half-life. Exclude padding from the mean and use only causal prefixes. Apply the same readout at the final real position during inference. This may retain broader interests while emphasizing recent behavior. Keep history truncation, embeddings, GRU topology, dot-product scoring, loss and optimizer fixed; update the predictor writer consistently.",
  "1": "Across all four datasets, factorize the shared item embedding table into item-specific 64-dimensional vectors and one jointly learned 64x128 projection, then retain unit normalization and exact zero padding. Use this same representation for history items and candidates. The lower-rank parameterization may improve statistical efficiency for sparse items without changing the 128-dimensional GRU interface. Preserve history policy, GRU topology, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer; update checkpoint loading and the predictor writer.",
  "2": "Across all four datasets, add a fixed residual path from the normalized item embedding to its corresponding GRU output: z_t = h_t + 0.25*e_t, using the embedding before input dropout. Apply this representation at existing supervised positions and the final-real-position inference readout. Direct access to the latest observed item may complement recurrent context when predicting short-term transitions. Preserve embedding parameterization, sequence policy, dot-product scoring, loss and optimizer. Retain one jointly trained GRU model per category and update the predictor writer.",
  "3": "Across all four datasets, compute item counts solely from training interactions and scale history embeddings by 1 + 0.1*clip(log1p(count[item])-median_positive_log_count, -2, 2). Use scale 1 for unseen items, keep padding zero and leave candidate embeddings unchanged. The earlier inverse-frequency scaling regressed; this tests whether a mild positive frequency signal improves recurrent inputs instead. Preserve learned embedding design, GRU topology, history selection, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer. Store the fixed feature buffer in each checkpoint and reproduce it through the predictor writer."
}
